It is ambiguous:

1) Is there anyone who can do it?  If there is even 1 person who
can the answer is yes.

2) Anyone can do it.  This is a claim that everyone willing to put
in the effort is able to do it, with the understanding that not
everyone will decide to put in the effort.

Can anyone do it?  Which case above is ambiguous.  This
ambiguity is used often in advertising - Commercials show the
most successful users of a product.  The example people say
"Anyone can do it".  The interpretation is left open between
the meanings in the two cases above.

1) Pick anyone.  Point that person at a patched version of
Nethack that starts a character with a wand of wishing.  What's
the chance this person will ascend the first try?  2) Now restrict
the list of possible anyone from anyone in general to players on
NAO andor posters on RGRN.  What's the chance this person
will ascend the first try?  3) Now restrict the list of possibilities
to folks who have already ascended at least once on the current
version 3.4.1.  What's the chance this person will ascend the
first try?  This list of progressive restrictions gives the best
description I can think of to the meaning of the original question -
Which anyone was meant?  Since the question was asked on
RGRN I think my second anyone above was meant.


